  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 254 AM CDT Tue Aug 18 2026\n\nAs of 2 AM CDT this morning, parts of our forecast area continue to \nsee a couple of storms tracking southeast across parts of south \ncentral SD. These have been associated with mid level shortwave \nenergy and an upper jet streak working out of the Northern High \nPlains into the Dakotas along with a weak sfc trough and an incoming \ncold front sagging south across ND. Current temperatures generally \nrange between 60-70 degrees with light winds.\n\nThe main forecast challenge during the next few days will continue \nto revolve around scattered storm chances as a northwesterly flow \naloft pattern remains persistent across the Northern Plains. Within \nthis flow, periodic shots of shortwave trough energy will rotate \nacross the region through mid week maintaining the active convective \npattern. What ends up being leftover from early morning storms \nacross parts of central and southeast SD along with shower activity \nfilling in across parts of northeast SD and west central MN will \nexit the forecast area by mid morning. Dry conditions are then \nexpected to set up for the rest of the day as a cold front shifts \nsouth across central and southern SD. This will help focus the \nbetter instability across southern sections of the state into NE \nduring peak heating this afternoon. However, parts of south central \nSD and the I-90 corridor will still see MLCAPE values of 1000-2000 \nJ/kg develop. A 100+ kt jet streak setting up across SD should help \nprovide adequate deep layer shear of 40-50 kts. This will be within \na post-frontal environment and the early morning convection that \nmoved through that area will have sent in motion an outflow boundary \nfarther south into NE and KS by this afternoon and evening. \nNevertheless, given that more shortwave energy will work out of the \nNorthern High Plains across parts of western SD, a couple of strong \nto severe storms cannot be ruled out this evening into early \nWednesday. SPC has maintained a Marginal Risk (level 1 of 5) for \nsevere weather across south central SD. Large hail to 1 inch in \ndiameter and damaging winds up to 60 mph will be the main threats.\n\nSfc high pressure will center itself across parts of ND during the \nday today and into eastern SD/western MN tonight into Wednesday \nmorning. This will provide for a cooler overnight with temperatures \nfalling back into the 50s. As the high slips off to our south and \neast during the day Wednesday, return flow will set up across \nwestern and central SD by the afternoon. During this time, sfc low \npressure along with a pseudo warm front or sfc trough will work back \nto the north and northeast Wednesday afternoon and Wednesday night. \nDaytime heating will lead to MLCAPE values of 1500-2500 J/kg with \ndeep layer shear values of 35 kts. Another round of showers and \nstorms are expected to develop in the afternoon and evening as \nanother shortwave trough is progged to move through the region. A \nfew storms could turn severe with large hail up to 1 inch in \ndiameter and damaging winds up to 60 mph. SPC has posted a Marginal \nRisk (level 1 of 5) for severe weather for central and northeast SD.\n\nThe outlook for the end of the week into the weekend will \nessentially feature more of the same. An upper ridge across the \nwestern CONUS will amplify as another sfc trough tracks southeast \nacross the eastern Dakotas and western MN on Thursday. Daytime \nheating will lead to another unstable environment with increasing \ninstability, adequate deep layer shear and steep lapse rates. It \nremains to be seen when and where any severe convection will set up \nbut our eastern zones will be in a more favorable window for some. \nHigh pressure building southeast into the region Friday into \nSaturday looks to give us a break from storms. Temperatures will \ncool a bit back just below normal and humidity values will return to \nmore comfortable ranges.",
